{"task_id": "HumanEval/4", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import List\n\n\ndef mean_absolute_deviation(numbers: List[float]) -> float:\n    \"\"\" For a given list of input numbers, calculate Mean Absolute Deviation\n    around the mean of this dataset. MAD is the average absolute difference\n    between each element and the mean.\n    \"\"\"\n    mean = sum(numbers) / len(numbers)\n    sum_abs_diffs = 0.0\n    for x in numbers:\n        sum_abs_diffs += abs(x - mean)\n    return sum_abs_diffs / len(numbers)\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/5", "solution": "from typing import List\n\n\ndef intersperse(numbers: List[int], delimeter: int) -> List[int]:\n    \"\"\" Insert a number 'delimeter' between every two consecutive elements of input list `numbers`.\n    The original list is not modified, and a new list is returned.\n    \"\"\"\n    if len(numbers) <= 1:\n        return list(numbers)\n\n    result: List[int] = []\n    for i, num in enumerate(numbers):\n        if i > 0:\n            result.append(delimeter)\n        result.append(num)\n    return result"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/5", "solution": "```python\n# pyproject.toml\n[build-system]\nrequires = [\"setuptools>=61.0\"]\nbuild-backend = \"setuptools.build_meta\"\n\n[project]\nname = \"intersperse-lib\"\nversion = \"0.1.0\"\ndescription = \"A library to intersperse a delimiter integer between consecutive integers in a list.\"\nreadme = \"README.md\"\nrequires-python = \">=3.9\"\ndependencies = [\n    \"fastapi~=0.104.0\",\n    \"uvicorn~=0.24.0\",\n    \"pydantic~=2.5.2\", # FastAPI dependency\n]\n\n[project.scripts]\nintersperse = \"intersperse_lib.cli:main\"\n\n[project.optional-dependencies]\ndev = [\n    \"pytest~=7.4.3\",\n    \"flake8~=6.1.0\",\n    \"mypy~=1.7.1\",\n    \"httpx~=0.25.1\", # For API testing\n]\n\n[tool.setuptools.packages.find]\nwhere = [\"src\"]\n```\n\n```python\n# README.md\n# intersperse-lib\n\nA Python library, CLI, and HTTP API for inserting a delimiter between consecutive integers in a list.\n\n## Installation\n\n```bash\npip install intersperse-lib\n# For CLI and API server functionality, install with dev extras:\npip install \"intersperse-lib[dev]\"\n```\n\n## Usage\n\n### Library\n\n```python\nfrom intersperse_lib import IntersperseService\nfrom intersperse_lib.core import IntersperseCore\nfrom intersperse_lib.validators import ListValidator\n\n# Dependency injection setup\nvalidator = ListValidator()\ncore = IntersperseCore()\nservice = IntersperseService(validator=validator, core=core)\n\n# Use the service\nresult = service.intersperse(numbers=[1, 2, 3], delimiter=0)\nprint(result)  # Output: [1, 0, 2, 0, 3]\n\ntry:\n    service.intersperse(numbers=[1, \"a\"], delimiter=0)\nexcept Exception as e:\n    print(f\"Error: {e}\") # Output: Error: element at index 1 is not an int\n```\n\n### CLI\n\n```bash\n# Using JSON string input\nintersperse '{\"numbers\":[10,20],\"delimiter\":-1}'\n# Output: {\"result\":[10,-1,20]}\n\n# Using positional arguments (comma-separated numbers, then delimiter)\nintersperse 1,2,3 0\n# Output: {\"result\":[1,0,2,0,3]}\n\n# Error case\nintersperse '{\"numbers\":[1,\"a\"],\"delimiter\":0}'\n# Output: {\"error\":\"ValidationError\",\"message\":\"element at index 1 is not an int\"} (to stderr, exit code 2)\n```\n\n### HTTP API\n\nStart the server:\n```bash\nuvicorn intersperse_lib.api:app --host 0.0.0.0 --port 8000\n```\n\nThen make a POST request:\n\n```bash\ncurl -X POST -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" \\\n     -d '{\"numbers\": [100, 200, 300], \"delimiter\": 99}' \\\n     http://localhost:8000/intersperse\n# Output: {\"result\":[100,99,200,99,300]}\n```\n\nError response (HTTP 400):\n```bash\ncurl -X POST -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" \\\n     -d '{\"numbers\": [100, \"oops\"], \"delimiter\": 99}' \\\n     http://localhost:8000/intersperse\n# Output: {\"detail\":{\"error\":\"ValidationError\",\"message\":\"element at index 1 is not an int\"}}\n```\n```\n\n```python\n# .github/workflows/ci.yml\nname: CI\n\non:\n  push:\n    branches: [ main ]\n  pull_request:\n    branches: [ main ]\n\njobs:\n  build:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    strategy:\n      matrix:\n        python-version: [\"3.9\", \"3.10\", \"3.11\"]\n\n    steps:\n    - uses: actions/checkout@v3\n    - name: Set up Python ${{ matrix.python-version }}\n      uses: actions/setup-python@v4\n      with:\n        python-version: ${{ matrix.python-version }}\n    - name: Install dependencies\n      run: |\n        python -m pip install --upgrade pip\n        pip install .[dev] # Install the package and dev dependencies\n    - name: Run tests\n      run: |\n        pytest\n    # - name: Run linting (optional)\n    #   run: |\n    #     flake8 src/intersperse_lib/\n    # - name: Run type checking (optional)\n    #   run: |\n    #     mypy src/intersperse_lib/\n```\n\n```python\n# src/intersperse_lib/__init__.py\n\"\"\"Intersperse library package.\"\"\"\nfrom .core import IntersperseCore\nfrom .validators import ListValidator\nfrom .services import IntersperseService\nfrom .errors import ValidationError\nfrom .cli import main # for pyproject.toml entry point\nfrom .api import app # for uvicorn entry point\n```\n\n```python\n# src/intersperse_lib/core.py\nfrom typing import List\n\nclass IntersperseCore:\n    \"\"\"Implements the pure algorithm that intersperses a delimiter between list elements.\"\"\"\n\n    @staticmethod\n    def intersperse(numbers: List[int], delimiter: int) -> List[int]:\n        \"\"\"Return a new list with a delimiter inserted between each consecutive pair.\"\"\"\n        if not numbers:\n            return []\n        out: List[int] = []\n        for i, v in enumerate(numbers):\n            out.append(v)\n            if i != len(numbers) - 1:\n                out.append(delimiter)\n        return out\n```\n\n```python\n# src/intersperse_lib/types.py\nfrom typing import List, Dict, Any\n\nclass IntersperseRequest:\n    \"\"\"DTO for intersperse requests.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, numbers: List[int], delimiter: int):\n        self.numbers = numbers\n        self.delimiter = delimiter\n\n    @classmethod\n    def from_dict(cls, data: Dict[str, Any]) -> \"IntersperseRequest\":\n        \"\"\"Create an instance from a dictionary, mapping keys.\"\"\"\n        return cls(\n            numbers=data[\"numbers\"],\n            delimiter=data[\"delimiter\"]\n        )\n\nclass IntersperseResponse:\n    \"\"\"DTO for intersperse responses.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, result: List[int]):\n        self.result = result\n\n    def to_dict(self) -> Dict[str, Any]:\n        \"\"\"Return a JSON-serializable representation.\"\"\"\n        return {\"result\": self.result}\n```\n\n```python\n# src/intersperse_lib/errors.py\nfrom typing import Dict, Any\n\nclass ValidationError(ValueError):\n    \"\"\"Custom exception for validation failures.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, message: str):\n        super().__init__(message)\n        self.message = message\n\ndef format_error(exc: Exception) -> Dict[str, Any]:\n    \"\"\"Convert an exception to a JSON-friendly error dictionary.\"\"\"\n    if isinstance(exc, ValidationError):\n        return {\"error\": \"ValidationError\", \"message\": exc.message}\n    return {\"error\": \"InternalError\", \"message\": \"An unexpected error occurred.\"}\n```\n\n```python\n# src/intersperse_lib/validators.py\nfrom typing import List\nfrom .errors import ValidationError\nfrom .types import IntersperseRequest\n\nclass ListValidator:\n    \"\"\"Validates input types and constraints.\"\"\"\n    MAX_LIST_LENGTH = 1_000_000\n\n    def validate_numbers(self, numbers: List[int]) -> None:\n        \"\"\"Ensure numbers is a list-like, length <= MAX_LIST_LENGTH, and every element is an int.\"\"\"\n        if not isinstance(numbers, list):\n            raise ValidationError(\"numbers must be a list of integers\")\n        if len(numbers) > self.MAX_LIST_LENGTH:\n            raise ValidationError(f\"numbers list too large, max length is {self.MAX_LIST_LENGTH}\")\n        for i, v in enumerate(numbers):\n            if not isinstance(v, int):\n                raise ValidationError(f\"element at index {i} is not an int\")\n\n    def validate_delimiter(self, delimiter: int) -> None:\n        \"\"\"Ensure delimiter is an integer.\"\"\"\n        if not isinstance(delimiter, int):\n            raise ValidationError(\"delimiter must be an integer\")\n\n    def validate_request(self, req: IntersperseRequest) -> None:\n        \"\"\"Validate an IntersperseRequest DTO.\"\"\"\n        self.validate_numbers(req.numbers)\n        self.validate_delimiter(req.delimiter)\n```\n\n```python\n# src/intersperse_lib/services.py\nfrom typing import List\nfrom .validators import ListValidator\nfrom .core import IntersperseCore\nfrom .types import IntersperseRequest\n\nclass IntersperseService:\n    \"\"\"Public service that composes validation and core algorithm.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, validator: ListValidator, core: IntersperseCore):\n        self._validator = validator\n        self._core = core\n\n    def intersperse(self, numbers: List[int], delimiter: int) -> List[int]:\n        \"\"\"Validate inputs and call core.intersperse, returning result.\"\"\"\n        request = IntersperseRequest(numbers=numbers, delimiter=delimiter)\n        self._validator.validate_request(request)\n        return self._core.intersperse(numbers, delimiter)\n```\n\n```python\n# src/intersperse_lib/cli.py\nimport argparse\nimport json\nimport sys\nfrom typing import List, Dict, Any\n\nfrom .services import IntersperseService\nfrom .validators import ListValidator\nfrom .core import IntersperseCore\nfrom .types import IntersperseRequest, IntersperseResponse\nfrom .errors import ValidationError, format_error\n\nEXIT_OK = 0\nEXIT_ERROR = 2 # For validation/usage errors, typically 2 for argparse\nEXIT_UNEXPECTED_ERROR = 1 # For other internal errors\n\nclass CLIController:\n    \"\"\"Command-line entrypoint for intersperse.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, service: IntersperseService):\n        self._service = service\n\n    def _parse_args(self, argv: List[str]) -> IntersperseRequest:\n        \"\"\"Parse CLI args and return DTO.\"\"\"\n        parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(\n            description=\"Intersperse a delimiter between numbers in a list.\",\n            epilog=\"Example: intersperse '{\\\"numbers\\\":[1,2],\\\"delimiter\\\":0}' or intersperse 1,2,3 0\"\n        )\n        parser.add_argument(\n            \"input\",\n            help=\"JSON string (e.g., '{\\\"numbers\\\":[1,2],\\\"delimiter\\\":0}') \"\n                 \"OR comma-separated numbers (e.g., '1,2,3').\"\n        )\n        parser.add_argument(\n            \"delimiter\",\n            nargs='?', # Optional, for positional mode\n            help=\"The integer delimiter. Required if numbers are provided as comma-separated list.\"\n        )\n\n        args = parser.parse_args(argv[1:])\n\n        numbers: List[int]\n        delimiter: int\n\n        if args.delimiter is not None: # Positional mode\n            try:\n                numbers_str = args.input.split(',')\n                numbers = [int(n.strip()) for n in numbers_str]\n                delimiter = int(args.delimiter)\n            except ValueError as e:\n                raise ValidationError(f\"Invalid input for numbers or delimiter in positional arguments: {e}\")\n        else: # JSON mode\n            try:\n                data = json.loads(args.input)\n                numbers = data[\"numbers\"]\n                delimiter = data[\"delimiter\"]\n            except json.JSONDecodeError as e:\n                raise ValidationError(f\"Invalid JSON input: {e}\")\n            except KeyError as e:\n                raise ValidationError(f\"Missing required key in JSON input: {e}\")\n        \n        return IntersperseRequest(numbers=numbers, delimiter=delimiter)\n\n\n    def run(self, argv: List[str]) -> int:\n        \"\"\"Accept argv, parse args, call service, print JSON result. Return exit code.\"\"\"\n        try:\n            request = self._parse_args(argv)\n            result_list = self._service.intersperse(request.numbers, request.delimiter)\n            response = IntersperseResponse(result=result_list)\n            print(json.dumps(response.to_dict()))\n            return EXIT_OK\n        except ValidationError as e:\n            print(json.dumps(format_error(e)), file=sys.stderr)\n            return EXIT_ERROR\n        except Exception as e:\n            print(json.dumps(format_error(e)), file=sys.stderr)\n            return EXIT_UNEXPECTED_ERROR\n\ndef main():\n    \"\"\"CLI entry point function.\"\"\"\n    validator = ListValidator()\n    core = IntersperseCore()\n    service = IntersperseService(validator=validator, core=core)\n    controller = CLIController(service=service)\n    sys.exit(controller.run(sys.argv))\n```\n\n```python\n# src/intersperse_lib/api.py\nfrom fastapi import FastAPI, HTTPException\nfrom typing import Dict, Any\n\nfrom .services import IntersperseService\nfrom .validators import ListValidator\nfrom .core import IntersperseCore\nfrom .types import IntersperseResponse\nfrom .errors import ValidationError, format_error\n\nclass APIController:\n    \"\"\"FastAPI HTTP wrapper for the service.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, service: IntersperseService):\n        self._service = service\n        self.app = self.create_app()\n\n    def create_app(self) -> FastAPI:\n        \"\"\"Build and return a FastAPI app with one route.\"\"\"\n        app = FastAPI(\n            title=\"Intersperse API\",\n            description=\"API for inserting a delimiter between numbers in a list.\",\n            version=\"0.1.0\"\n        )\n\n        @app.post(\"/intersperse\")\n        async def intersperse_endpoint(payload: Dict[str, Any]):\n            \"\"\"Convert payload to DTO, call service.intersperse, return dict with result.\"\"\"\n            try:\n                numbers = payload.get(\"numbers\")\n                delimiter = payload.get(\"delimiter\")\n\n                if numbers is None or delimiter is None:\n                    # FastAPI's default request body parsing will often catch this earlier,\n                    # but adding explicit check for robustness.\n                    raise ValidationError(\"Missing 'numbers' or 'delimiter' in request body.\")\n\n                result_list = self._service.intersperse(numbers, delimiter)\n                return IntersperseResponse(result=result_list).to_dict()\n            except ValidationError as e:\n                raise HTTPException(status_code=400, detail=format_error(e))\n            except Exception as e:\n                # Log the unexpected error here for debugging purposes\n                raise HTTPException(status_code=500, detail=format_error(e))\n        return app\n\n# Global instance for uvicorn to pick up\nvalidator = ListValidator()\ncore = IntersperseCore()\nservice = IntersperseService(validator=validator, core=core)\napi_controller = APIController(service=service)\napp = api_controller.app\n```\n\n```python\n# docs/api.md\n# Intersperse HTTP API Documentation\nThis document describes the HTTP API for the `intersperse-lib` functionality.\n\n## Base URL\nThe API is served by a FastAPI application. The base URL will depend on how the application is deployed (e.g., `http://localhost:8000`).\n\n## Endpoint: `POST /intersperse`\n\nInserts a specified delimiter integer between each pair of consecutive integers in an input list.\n\n### Request\n\n- **Method**: `POST`\n- **Path**: `/intersperse`\n- **Content-Type**: `application/json`\n\n#### Request Body Schema\n\n| Field       | Type           | Description                                       | Required |\n|-------------|----------------|---------------------------------------------------|----------|\n| `numbers`   | `List[int]`    | An array of integers to be processed.             | Yes      |\n| `delimiter` | `int`          | The integer to insert between consecutive numbers.| Yes      |\n\n**Example Request Body:**\n\n```json\n{\n  \"numbers\": [10, 20, 30],\n  \"delimiter\": 99\n}\n```\n\n### Response\n\n- **Content-Type**: `application/json`\n\n#### Success Response (HTTP 200 OK)\n\n**Response Body Schema:**\n\n| Field    | Type           | Description                                  |\n|----------|----------------|----------------------------------------------|\n| `result` | `List[int]`    | The list of integers with the delimiter inserted. |\n\n**Example Success Response:**\n\n```json\n{\n  \"result\": [10, 99, 20, 99, 30]\n}\n```\n\n#### Error Response (HTTP 400 Bad Request)\n\nReturned when the input `numbers` list or `delimiter` is invalid (e.g., wrong type, missing fields).\n\n**Response Body Schema:**\n\n| Field     | Type      | Description                                  |\n|-----------|-----------|----------------------------------------------|\n| `detail`  | `object`  | An object containing error details.          |\n| `detail.error` | `string`  | The type of error (e.g., \"ValidationError\"). |\n| `detail.message` | `string`  | A human-readable message describing the error. |\n\n**Example 400 Error Response:**\n\n```json\n{\n  \"detail\": {\n    \"error\": \"ValidationError\",\n    \"message\": \"element at index 1 is not an int\"\n  }\n}\n```\n\n#### Server Error Response (HTTP 500 Internal Server Error)\n\nReturned for unexpected server-side issues.\n\n**Response Body Schema:**\n\n| Field     | Type      | Description                                  |\n|-----------|-----------|----------------------------------------------|\n| `detail`  | `object`  | An object containing error details.          |\n| `detail.error` | `string`  | The type of error (e.g., \"InternalError\"). |\n| `detail.message` | `string`  | A generic error message. |\n\n**Example 500 Error Response:**\n\n```json\n{\n  \"detail\": {\n    \"error\": \"InternalError\",\n    \"message\": \"An unexpected error occurred.\"\n  }\n}\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/4", "solution": "from typing import List\n\n\ndef mean_absolute_deviation(numbers: List[float]) -> float:\n    \"\"\"For a given list of input numbers, calculate Mean Absolute Deviation\n    around the mean of this dataset. Mean Absolute Deviation is the average\n    absolute difference between each element and a centerpoint (mean in this case).\n    \"\"\"\n    n = len(numbers)\n    # The design specifies validation and error handling, but critical requirements forbid\n    # raising exceptions for edge cases and trust caller's inputs.\n    # Therefore, direct computation is performed, allowing native ZeroDivisionError for empty list.\n    mean = sum(numbers) / n\n    total_abs_deviation = sum(abs(x - mean) for x in numbers)\n    mad = total_abs_deviation / n\n    return mad"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/6", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import List\n\n\ndef parse_nested_parens(paren_string: str) -> List[int]:\n    \"\"\"Calculates the deepest nesting level for groups of parentheses.\n    Groups are separated by spaces, and the function returns a list of depths for each group.\"\"\"\n    results = []\n    # Split the input string by whitespace to get individual groups.\n    groups = paren_string.split()\n\n    for group in groups:\n        current_depth = 0\n        max_depth = 0\n        # Iterate through each character in the group to find the max nesting depth.\n        for char in group:\n            if char == '(':\n                current_depth += 1\n                if current_depth > max_depth:\n                    max_depth = current_depth\n            elif char == ')':\n                current_depth -= 1\n        results.append(max_depth)\n    return results\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/6", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import List\n\n\ndef parse_nested_parens(paren_string: str) -> List[int]:\n    \"\"\" Input to this function is a string represented multiple groups for nested parentheses separated by spaces.\n    For each of the group, output the deepest level of nesting of parentheses.\n    E.g. (()()) has maximum two levels of nesting while ((())) has three.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not paren_string:\n        return []\n\n    groups = paren_string.split()\n    results = []\n\n    for group in groups:\n        current_depth = 0\n        max_depth_for_group = 0\n        for char in group:\n            if char == '(':\n                current_depth += 1\n            else:\n                current_depth -= 1\n            max_depth_for_group = max(max_depth_for_group, current_depth)\n        results.append(max_depth_for_group)\n\n    return results\n```"}
